    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners on flat or rolling courses who want a wide deck and a strong warranty with no membership, from Amazon.",
    description: "The Horizon 7.8 AT, discussed in this guide, is sold by Horizon and specialist retailers and we could not find it on Amazon US, so this card shows its Studio Series sibling, which Amazon does sell. The 7.4 AT shares the 7.8 AT’s 22 by 60-inch deck, 0.5 to 12 mph and 0 to 15% incline; Horizon lists a 350 lb maximum user weight, one-step hydraulic folding and an 8.75-inch step-on height, and a Johnson Fitness and Wellness listing and independent coverage agree on the deck, speed, incline and capacity. The console is an 8.25-inch LCD with a 16-digit LED message line and ten programs including Sprint 8, Horizon includes a Bluetooth heart rate monitor, and Bluetooth FTMS lets running apps read it with nothing to subscribe to. The warranty is the same strong one as the 7.8 AT: five years on parts and two of labour alongside lifetime frame and motor cover. What you give up against the 7.8 AT is 25 lb of user rating, the colour display and the thicker belt, and we could not confirm that the 7.4 AT’s console shows pace, which the 7.8 AT’s does; on a long run you can read pace from a watch or app instead. On 13 October 2026 Horizon’s store listed it at $1,799, or $1,499 with a promotional code. One independent review quotes a 3.5 CHP motor; Horizon does not publish a figure, so we left it out.",
  },
  {
    id: "aviron-summit",
    name: "Aviron Summit Treadmill",
    brand: "Aviron",
    category: "Best Treadmill for Downhill Courses on Amazon",
    badge: "Most Decline",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HGN8NNDK?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51HkwftQdqL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,999-$3,597 at Aviron on 20 Oct 2026; $2,849-$3,148 on Amazon on 9 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: {"low":2849,"high":3597},
    specs: "22 x 61 in. deck · 4.5 CHP · -6% to 42% incline · 350 lb max user · 364 lb · does not fold",
    pros: [
      "Decline to -6%, the same as the NordicTrack X16 and twice that of the NordicTrack Commercial models",
      "Incline to 42% for steep climbing work in the same block",
      "A 22 by 61-inch deck and a 4.5 CHP motor, figures Aviron and two independent sources agree on",
      "A 350 lb user rating on a non-folding frame",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not fold and weighs 364 lb",
      "Top speed falls as the grade rises, and sources disagree on the exact top speed, so it is left off the card",
      "Aviron recommends an 8 ft ceiling to use the full incline range",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners preparing for a course with long descents, who have a permanent spot for a non-folding machine.",
    description: "The NordicTrack X16, which this guide discusses for downhill training, could not be found on Amazon US, so this card shows the closest incline-and-decline trainer that Amazon does sell. Aviron’s Summit goes downhill as far as the X16, to -6%, and climbs to 42%. Aviron, Garage Gym Reviews and Wellworthy agree on a 4.5 CHP motor, that -6% to 42% grade range and a 22 by 61-inch belt, and Aviron and Garage Gym Reviews agree on a 350 lb user limit, a 364 lb machine weight and dimensions of 67 by 37 by 71 inches; it does not fold and moves on transport wheels. Aviron gives a 14 mph top speed but limits it by grade, and Garage Gym Reviews’ tables give 0.5 to 14.5 and 0 to 14 mph, so top speed is left off the card; at the grades a marathoner uses, that limit will not bite. Six percent of decline lets you rehearse the steeper descents of hilly road courses. Its screens run Aviron’s own app, and Garage Gym Reviews notes owner reports that the larger screens can shake at higher speeds. On 9 November 2026 its Amazon listing showed $2,849 with the 16-inch screen and $3,148 with the 22-inch.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-2450",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
    brand: "NordicTrack",
    category: "Best Marathon Treadmill for Speed Work",
    badge: "Most Speed",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D5YKDXDV?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31NVjoFCMQL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$2,499–$2,999",
    priceBounds: {"low":2499,"high":2999},
    specs: "22 x 60 in. belt · 0–14 mph · -3% to 12% incline · 4.25 CHP · 400 lb capacity · 24 in. pivoting screen",
    pros: [
      "14 mph leaves room for strides and fast intervals in the same machine",
      "The same 4.25 CHP rating, 22 x 60-inch belt and 400 lb capacity as the 1750",
      "Decline to -3% and an AutoBreeze fan",
      "A 24-inch screen for following routes on long runs, with an iFIT membership",
    ],
    cons: [
      "About $500 more than the 1750 for speed most marathoners will not use",
      "Much of the console is built around iFIT, a paid membership — check the current price",
      "Two years on parts and one year of labour",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For fast marathoners whose speed work goes beyond 12 mph, and for anyone who wants the biggest screen for long runs.",
    description: "The Commercial 2450 is the 1750 with more speed and a bigger screen, and for most marathoners that is not worth the extra money. For fast runners, it is. NordicTrack lists 0 to 14 mph, incline from 12% down to -3% decline, a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor rating, a 400 lb user capacity and an AutoBreeze fan, and Treadmill Review Guru’s review agrees on each figure. That 14 mph is a 4:17-per-mile pace, which matters for strides and short intervals rather than for marathon pace itself. The 24-inch pivoting touchscreen runs iFIT, which with a paid membership plays scenic routes and adjusts speed and incline for you; it is the most immersive screen here for a long run. The warranty is lifetime on the motor, ten years on the frame, two years on parts and one year of labour. NordicTrack was selling it at $2,499.99 against a $2,999.99 list price. If your fastest sessions stay under 12 mph, buy the 1750.",
  },
  {
    id: "sole-tt8",
    name: "Sole TT8",
    brand: "Sole",
    category: "Best Premium Treadmill for High Mileage",
    badge: "Best Premium",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B003Z4G76Y?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41PV053eTOL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $2,500 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":2500,"high":2500},
    specs: "4.0 HP · 22 x 60 in. · 0.5–12 mph · -6% to 15% incline · 400 lb capacity · non-folding · 15.6 in. touchscreen",
    pros: [
      "Non-folding frame with a 400 lb capacity, per SOLE",
      "22 x 60 in. running surface for long runs when your stride changes late on",
      "Decline to -6%, useful for downhill race courses",
      "Programs including a 10K, HIIT and heart-rate control, per the listing, with no subscription needed",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not fold, and weighs more than 300 lb",
      "Tops out at 12 mph",
      "Published warranty terms differ between sources; check the current terms before buying",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For marathoners logging 40 or more miles a week who have the room for a non-folding treadmill and want no subscription.",
    description: "The Wahoo KICKR RUN, the course-simulation treadmill this guide discusses, is sold by Wahoo and not on Amazon, so the premium card here is the machine Amazon sells that is built most clearly for heavy, repeated use: SOLE’s light-commercial TT8. The Amazon listing gives a 4.0 HP motor, a 22 x 60 in. running surface, a 0.5 to 12 mph speed range, an incline and decline range of -6 to 15 and a 15.6 in. touch screen, with programs including Hill, Fat Burn, HIIT, 10K and heart-rate control. SOLE’s own site adds a non-folding frame and a 400 lb user capacity. For a high-mileage runner, the non-folding frame and high capacity are what matter: a frame that does not fold has no hinge to flex, and a machine rated well above your weight works less hard on every stride over thousands of miles. The decline is useful too, for runners training for downhill courses. It will not replay a race course the way the KICKR RUN does, and it weighs more than 300 lb, so place it once and leave it. It stops at 12 mph, which is ample for long runs and most speed work. Amazon listed it at about $2,500 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-7-0-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.0 AT",
    brand: "Horizon",
    category: "Best Budget Treadmill for Marathon Training",
