        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. The picks were made from manufacturer product pages and specification tables,
            warranty terms, retailer listings and independent review coverage, all checked on 15
            October 2026. Our reviews of the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</a> and the Horizon 7.0 AT are specification reviews too, not hands-on tests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Six rules decided the list. <strong>On sale now</strong> on the maker's own store, under
            the name we use. <strong>A full running deck:</strong> 60 inches long at least, because
            a long run magnifies every compromise in stride length. <strong>A motor built for
            continuous work</strong>, judged by a published continuous rating, a long motor
            warranty or both. <strong>A useful top speed:</strong> at least 12 mph, which leaves
            room for faster sessions in the same training block. <strong>Agreement</strong> on deck
            size, speed range, incline range and weight capacity between the maker and at least one
            independent source. And <strong>no safety action</strong> in the US Consumer Product
            Safety Commission's recall database for the model.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some strong running treadmills are missing on purpose. Matrix's retail T30, T50 and T75
            are named in a CPSC recall announced in January 2026 over a power cord that can work
            loose and overheat — a particular concern on a machine running for hours — so Matrix is
            not here. SOLE's long-running favourites for distance runners, the <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>F80</a> and F85, were
            redesigned for 2026 with different motors, decks and capacities under the same names,
            and most independent coverage still describes the earlier versions; until it catches
            up we could not cross-check enough figures to list them. Our <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SOLE F85 review</a> sets out which figures conflict. SOLE's TT8, a decline-capable model, showed as
            unavailable on SOLE's own store when we checked.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon's T101 was recalled in 2022 because it could change speed or stop on its own,
            and the Peloton Tread was recalled in 2021 over a touchscreen that could detach. Neither
            is on this list, and none of the Horizon models we do list is covered by the T101
            recall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-and-cooling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Motor and Cooling for Two- and Three-Hour Runs</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill motor working for three hours does not fail because of the peak demand of a
            single stride. It fails, when it does, because of heat: sustained current through the
            windings, a belt dragging on a dry deck, and a motor compartment full of dust. A
            marathon machine needs a motor with headroom, so it is not working near its limit for
            hours, and an owner who keeps the friction down.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Read the rating, then the warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Look for a continuous rating, written CHP. NordicTrack rates the Commercial 1750,
            Commercial 2450 and X16 at 4.25 CHP, and independent sources agree. NordicTrack's own
            footnote is worth reading: it says the figure is measured in laboratory testing on a
            dynamometer and that in ordinary household use the motor does not operate at the
            horsepower shown. That is honest, and it is true of every treadmill rating. Horizon
            publishes no horsepower figure for the 7.0 AT or 7.8 AT, but it does publish a lifetime
            motor warranty, as NordicTrack does for its machines. For a marathoner, the warranty is
            the more useful number: it is the manufacturer's own estimate of how long the motor
            will last under normal use. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> and our comparison of <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC and DC treadmill motors</a> explain the ratings in more depth.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Keep the friction down</h3>
          <p>
            Long runs make maintenance matter more. A belt that needs lubricating forces the motor
            to work harder on every stride for the whole session, and that is the commonest route to
            a treadmill running hot. Follow the lubrication schedule in your manual, which is often
            a condition of the warranty, and vacuum under and around the machine regularly. Our
            guides to <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication</a> and <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill overheating</a> cover both. If a manual sets a limit on continuous use, respect it, and if you notice a
            burning smell or the motor cutting out late in a long run, stop and investigate rather
            than finishing the session.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power for a long session</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill drawing current for three hours deserves a proper outlet. Horizon's
            specification table says its AT treadmills are not compatible with GFCI outlets, and
            Wahoo says the KICKR RUN needs a dedicated circuit and a non-GFCI outlet. Check your
            manual's requirements before you choose a spot, avoid extension leads, and read our
            guide to <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill surge protectors</a> before plugging into one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-cushioning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Deck Length, Width and the Cushioning Trade-Off</h2>
<p>
            Sixty inches is the standard length for a running treadmill, and for marathon training
            it is a minimum rather than a luxury. Late in a long run your form changes: stride
            lengthens or shortens, you drift forward and back, and attention wanders. A short deck
            punishes all of that. Every machine on a card here has a 60-inch running area, and so
            does the Wahoo KICKR RUN, whose exact deck size is quoted differently by different
            sources but never under 60 inches. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> explains the lengths and widths for different heights and paces, and taller runners
            should also read our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for tall runners</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Width matters more on long runs than most people expect. On a 20-inch belt, such as the
            Horizon 7.0 AT's, you need to hold a straighter line than on a 22-inch belt, and over two
            or three hours that is a small but constant demand on concentration. If you can afford
            the wider deck, it is worth it for distance work.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cushioning: softer is not always better</h3>
          <p>
            Cushioned decks are a reason many runners do some of their long runs indoors, and all
            six machines here have them. But cushioning is a trade-off, not a free gift. A very
            soft deck absorbs some of the energy you would otherwise get back, so the same pace can
            feel harder, and it feels different from the road you will race on. Some runners use a
            softer deck for recovery runs and long, easy mileage and want something firmer for
            race-pace work. Horizon describes its three-zone cushioning as giving different amounts
            of flex through each phase of the stride. Our page on <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a> explains the systems.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the deck, it does not replace running shoes suited to the distance. Shoes wear
            out faster than most runners think when mileage climbs, and our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-replace-running-shoes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">when to replace running shoes</a> is worth reading during a marathon block.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pace-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Speed Headroom and a Console That Shows Pace</h2>
<p>
            Marathon pace itself does not need a fast treadmill. The table below shows the belt
            speed for common finishing times; even a sub-three-hour marathon runs at under 9 mph.
            Headroom matters for the rest of the training block: strides, tempo runs and intervals
            run well above marathon pace, and a machine that tops out at 10 mph leaves faster
            runners short.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Marathon finish</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pace per mile</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Belt speed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2:45</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6:18</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9.5 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6:52</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8.7 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3:30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8:01</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.5 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9:09</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6.6 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4:30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10:18</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.8 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">11:27</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.2 mph</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The figures are arithmetic over 26.2 miles, rounded. Our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a> has every conversion. Twelve mph, a five-minute mile, is enough for almost every
            marathoner's speed work. The <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 2450</a>'s 14 mph and the KICKR RUN's 4:00-per-mile
            top speed matter only to quite fast runners.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pace on the console</h3>
          <p>
            Marathoners think in minutes per mile; most treadmills think in miles per hour. A
            console that shows pace saves you converting in your head for three hours. Check this on
            the exact model, because it varies even within one range: Horizon's own comparison table
            lists pace among the 7.8 AT's workout readouts but not among the 7.0 AT's. NordicTrack's
            touchscreens and the KICKR RUN, which Wahoo describes in pace terms throughout, give you
            more ways to view the data, but confirm the field you want is shown in the mode you
            will actually use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treadmill speed is also not always accurate. If your long runs are paced to the second,
            read our guides to <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">checking treadmill speed</a> and <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration</a>,
            or pace with a foot pod you trust outdoors as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Decline for Downhill-Heavy Courses</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmills only go up. For a flat marathon that hardly matters, but some courses
            have long descents, and running downhill uses your legs differently: the muscles work
            while lengthening to brake each stride, and that is the kind of load that leaves legs
            sore late in a downhill race. A treadmill with powered decline lets you practise it
            indoors. Our guide to <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmill decline is good for</a> covers the reasons in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four machines here have decline. The NordicTrack Commercial 1750 and 2450 reach -3%
            (as does the Wahoo KICKR RUN, which Wahoo sells direct); the NordicTrack X16 (not sold on Amazon US), the Aviron
            Summit and the Sole TT8 reach -6%. Three percent is a gentle
            but genuine descent, enough to practise downhill running on most courses. Six percent is
            closer to the steepest stretches of the hilliest road races. The two Horizons have no
            decline at all. Our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a> compares every option we could verify.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. Downhill running is harder on the legs than it feels at the time, so
            build it in gradually and in short segments rather than adding long downhill blocks to
            your longest runs straight away. And if you have knee or other joint problems, ask a
            physiotherapist or doctor whether downhill work suits you before you buy a machine for
            it. We review equipment; this is not medical advice.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your race is flat, do not pay for decline you will not use. The money is better spent
            on a wider deck or a better warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fans-and-airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Fans, Airflow and Staying Cool</h2>
<p>
            Heat is the most underestimated problem of long treadmill runs. Outdoors, moving air
            cools you as you run; on a treadmill you are stationary in still air, and a two-hour run
            in a small room turns it warm and humid, which is one reason the same pace can feel
            harder indoors than outside.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every machine here has some answer. The Horizons have console fans. NordicTrack's
            AutoBreeze fan, on all three NordicTracks here, adjusts its speed with the intensity of
            your workout. Wahoo says its separately sold KICKR Headwind fan aligns with the KICKR
            RUN's open console and can follow your heart rate. Be realistic about built-in fans,
            though: they are small and aimed at your chest and face, and on a long run most people
            want more air than they provide.
          </p>
          <p>
            A floor or pedestal fan positioned in front of and slightly to the side of the
            treadmill is the single most useful accessory for marathon training indoors. Add cross-
            ventilation if you can, keep the room cool before you start rather than trying to cool
            it during the run, and keep a drink within reach of the handrail. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-stay-cool-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">staying cool on a treadmill</a> and the <a href="/best-treadmill-fans/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill fans</a> go into detail. If you feel dizzy, confused or stop sweating during a hot session, stop
            running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-run-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Setting Up for Long Treadmill Runs</h2>
<p>
            The machine is only half of a good long run indoors. A few set-up decisions make the
            difference between a session you can repeat every week and one you dread.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Placement.</strong> Put the treadmill where air can move, not in a closed
              box room, and leave the clearance behind it that your manual specifies. Wahoo, for
              example, specifies clearance behind and on each side of the KICKR RUN. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-put-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to put a treadmill</a> covers the trade-offs.</li>
            <li><strong>A mat.</strong> It protects the floor, keeps dust away from the motor and
              cuts noise for the neighbours over a long session. See the <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Fuel and fluids within reach.</strong> Set up the drink and gels you plan to
              race with where you can reach them without stepping off. Practising race-day fuelling
              is one of the genuine advantages of a treadmill long run.</li>
            <li><strong>Something to watch or follow.</strong> Long runs indoors are mentally hard.
              A tablet holder, a route on screen or a structured session helps; our tips on <a href="/how-to-make-treadmill-running-less-boring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making treadmill running less boring</a> are aimed at exactly this.</li>
            <li><strong>Vary the grade.</strong> Running at a fixed setting for hours is unlike any
              road. Small changes of incline through a long run break the monotony, and a course
              profile does it for you on machines that support one.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety key.</strong> Clip it on for long runs as for any other. Fatigue
              late in a run is when people drift back.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Splitting long runs between road and treadmill, rather than doing them all indoors, keeps
            the legs used to the surface you will race on. Our <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">marathon training guide</a> discusses how to combine the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            These are full running treadmills, and not every runner training for a long race needs
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will only do the odd run indoors</strong> when the weather is bad, a
            marathon-grade machine is a lot to spend. A good everyday running treadmill with a
            60-inch deck will cope with occasional long runs; see the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a> and the <a href="/best-treadmills-under-2000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $2,000</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are training for a half marathon or a first 10K,</strong> the same
            principles apply but the stakes are lower; a shorter long run puts much less sustained
            load on the motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your training is mostly fast intervals,</strong> control layout and top
            speed matter more than motor endurance and decline; our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for HIIT</a> is the better starting point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 300 lb,</strong> look closely at capacity. The
            NordicTrack machines and the Sole TT8 here are rated to 400 lb and the Horizon 7.8 AT to
            375 lb, but Wahoo’s KICKR RUN is rated to only 250 lb. Long runs put more cumulative load on the deck and
            motor than short ones, so a wide margin above your own weight is sensible; our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a> covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for Marathon Training</h2>
          <p>Each of these comes from choosing a treadmill for a 30-minute run and then asking it to do three hours.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Judging the motor by its peak rating</h3>
              <p>A big peak horsepower figure says nothing about how a motor copes with hours of continuous work. Look for a continuous rating and, above all, the length of the motor warranty. A lifetime motor warranty from a maker that has been selling treadmills for years tells you more than any number printed on the hood.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Settling for a short deck</h3>
              <p>A 55-inch deck feels fine for a 20-minute test in a shop. Two hours into a long run, when your stride has changed and your attention is wandering, it is a constant problem. For marathon training, treat 60 inches as the minimum and 22 inches of width as worth paying for.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Relying on the built-in fan</h3>
              <p>Console fans are small and aimed at your face. On a long run in a closed room they are rarely enough, and the heat that builds up makes a steady pace feel harder than it should. Budget for a proper floor fan and ventilation alongside the treadmill, and set them up before the first long run.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping maintenance during the block</h3>
              <p>The months of a marathon block are when a treadmill does the most work, and when owners are least inclined to spend time on it. A dry belt or a dusty motor compartment makes the motor run hotter on every stride. Lubricate and vacuum on the schedule in your manual, especially before the longest runs.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For most marathoners, the <strong class="text-white">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong> is the treadmill to buy: a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor rating with a lifetime
            motor warranty, decline to -3% and an automatic fan for about $2,000. If you want no
            subscription and a clear pace readout, the <strong class="text-white">Horizon 7.8
            AT</strong> is the alternative, or its sibling the 7.4 AT on Amazon, and if your race goes
            steeply downhill, the <strong class="text-white">Aviron Summit</strong> and <strong class="text-white">Sole TT8</strong> go to -6%, as does the NordicTrack X16 sold
            direct.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, the room matters as much as the machine on a three-hour run: air,
            power and a drink within reach. For how to structure the long runs themselves, our <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill marathon training guide</a> is the next step.
          </p>
        </div>

