        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for marathon training?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most runners, one with a 60-inch deck, a continuous motor rating with a long motor warranty, a 12 mph top speed and ideally some decline. Of the machines we could verify, the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 fits best, with a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP rating, -3% decline and an automatic fan. The Horizon 7.8 AT is the pick if you want no subscription; on Amazon, its sibling the 7.4 AT has the same deck and warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you train for a marathon on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, many runners do much of their training indoors, especially in winter or heat. A treadmill is good for controlled pace, practising fuelling and safe running in the dark. Keeping some long runs on the road helps your legs stay used to the surface and the camber you will race on. Our marathon training guide covers how to combine the two.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a treadmill handle a three-hour run?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A well-built running treadmill should, if it is maintained. Long runs load the motor continuously, so look for a continuous motor rating and a long motor warranty, keep the belt lubricated on schedule and keep the motor compartment clean. Check your manual for any limit on continuous use, and stop if you notice a burning smell or the motor cutting out.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do you need decline on a treadmill for marathon training?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if your race has significant descents. Downhill running loads the legs differently, and decline lets you practise it indoors. Most machines with decline go to -3%, which is enough for gentle descents; the NordicTrack X16, the Aviron Summit and the Sole TT8 go to -6%. For a flat course, decline is a nice extra rather than a need, and the money may be better spent on a wider deck or warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What size treadmill deck do you need for long runs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At least 60 inches long, and 22 inches wide if you can afford it. Your stride and position on the belt change as you tire late in a long run, and a short or narrow deck makes you concentrate on staying centred for hours. Taller runners and faster runners benefit most from the extra room. A 55-inch deck is fine for walking and jogging but not for marathon training.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is 12 mph fast enough for marathon training?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For almost everyone, yes. Twelve mph is a five-minute mile, and even a sub-three-hour marathon is run at under 9 mph. The extra speed matters for strides and short intervals, and only quite fast runners reach 12 mph in those. If you do, the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 reaches 14 mph, and Wahoo’s KICKR RUN, sold direct by Wahoo, a four-minute-mile pace.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

